title: Freezing metadata by not watching
tags: [[File tracking]]
kind: rationale
summary: Turning watching off on a subtree is how you freeze its metarecords against outside changes, so nothing writes metadata there to make up for the missing watcher.

A tracked file is, by construction, watched: `mf_watch` is inherited down the tree.
Turning watching off on a subtree is therefore a ''deliberate'' act — it is how you
''freeze'' its metarecords against outside processes (moves, deletions, rewrites) that
would otherwise change their metadata: an archive you want recorded as it was, a
volume another tool reorganises.

So no part of metafolder writes metadata for an unwatched subtree ''to compensate'' for
the missing watcher — setting `mfr_path` to `nothing` because "the repository is not
watched, so someone has to", say. That would override exactly what the user asked for.
When watching is on, the watcher does the work; when it is off, the metadata stays as it
is, and a later reconcile or re-watching catches up when you choose.

The limit is a request about the metarecord itself: trashing a file removes its
metarecord even outside the watched area, because trashing is a request about the
metarecord, not a tool guessing in the watcher's place ([[What trashing does]]).
